1
00:00:04,362 --> 00:00:14,312
[SPEAKER_01]: One thing that I've been thinking about is the notion of going too tight or too loose.

2
00:00:15,513 --> 00:00:17,515
[SPEAKER_01]: And it seems to me that it's more that

3
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:21,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Too tight.

4
00:00:22,021 --> 00:00:26,904
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like you're on one spectrum and you're just too tight or you're too loose.

5
00:00:28,225 --> 00:00:39,592
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're too tight, that means that you're going to be too loose where you should be tighter and you're too tight where you should be looser.

6
00:00:42,894 --> 00:00:46,737
[SPEAKER_01]: But then it will be the case that if it's too much,

7
00:00:51,013 --> 00:00:56,676
[SPEAKER_01]: There's something that you're not seeing that you should be restraining, and everything else is just kind of secondary to that.

8
00:00:58,617 --> 00:00:59,117
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yes.

9
00:00:59,698 --> 00:01:01,098
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

10
00:01:02,239 --> 00:01:04,220
[SPEAKER_02]: What is it, then, that you wouldn't be seeing?

11
00:01:09,303 --> 00:01:12,024
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody's restraining too much, what is it that they're overlooking?

12
00:01:12,724 --> 00:01:18,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Your, I guess, your incessant tendency to keep saying other things.

13
00:01:18,528 --> 00:01:18,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

14
00:01:19,269 --> 00:01:20,269
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're giving in to that.

15
00:01:20,489 --> 00:01:21,730
[SPEAKER_02]: You should really restrain that.

16
00:01:22,451 --> 00:01:33,318
[SPEAKER_02]: Restrain your giving in to that, to that mental pressure, to double down and say no to more things, just because it's pressuring you that way.

17
00:01:37,421 --> 00:01:39,683
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what's being overlooked as a result of that

18
00:01:41,123 --> 00:01:49,286
[SPEAKER_02]: One is restraining too much, saying no to stuff that has nothing to do with anything at all, really.

19
00:01:51,267 --> 00:01:56,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your criteria, well, it's still going to be just this doesn't feel nice.

20
00:01:56,648 --> 00:02:00,130
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I try harder, then I'll feel better about myself.

21
00:02:00,510 --> 00:02:01,970
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah.

22
00:02:02,090 --> 00:02:06,772
[SPEAKER_02]: If I stick to this long enough, this not feeling nice will go away.

23
00:02:09,393 --> 00:02:09,913
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's like,

24
00:02:11,665 --> 00:02:12,766
[SPEAKER_02]: It will go away either way.

25
00:02:15,767 --> 00:02:20,770
[SPEAKER_02]: As an arisen mental pressure or state, it will go away.

26
00:02:21,250 --> 00:02:24,072
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to recognize, okay, why it has arisen.

27
00:02:24,792 --> 00:02:26,313
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it because something I've done or something?

28
00:02:26,333 --> 00:02:34,377
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, no, there's just a mental tendency to... okay, the more... if there is like... if I say no to more things, the quicker it will be.

29
00:02:34,517 --> 00:02:35,098
[SPEAKER_02]: Quicker what?

30
00:02:35,298 --> 00:02:36,458
[SPEAKER_02]: What will... what will be quicker?

31
00:02:37,219 --> 00:02:39,440
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, my running away from suffering will be quicker.

32
00:02:40,939 --> 00:02:50,346
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, well, the whole practice is about not being moved by it, not being faster than it, not making it disappear as quickly as possible, which means I want it to go away.

33
00:02:51,267 --> 00:03:00,014
[SPEAKER_02]: The attitude is, I don't want this, I crave against it, let me do these practices, they will remove it for me.

34
00:03:00,034 --> 00:03:05,478
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another thing that I was thinking about along those lines is, I was always kind of like,

35
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:10,382
[SPEAKER_01]: I console myself with, like, oh, at least I have, you know, a sense of shame.

36
00:03:11,502 --> 00:03:20,687
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I'm, like, always trying to be better and improve, but realizing that's one of the, um, itis.

37
00:03:21,728 --> 00:03:26,030
[SPEAKER_01]: That's one of the, like, spiritual powers of the noble disciples.

38
00:03:27,931 --> 00:03:33,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you're at that point, you don't have the criteria to distinguish between, well,

39
00:03:35,022 --> 00:03:39,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, you're going to be feeling guilty about things you shouldn't be, and you're not going to be feeling guilty about things you should.

40
00:03:42,666 --> 00:03:47,529
[SPEAKER_01]: And mistaking that for, like, actual sense of shame.

41
00:03:48,450 --> 00:03:48,690
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

42
00:03:48,710 --> 00:04:03,699
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that can be... Well, that's... it's... that kind of irrational side of the mind, that would feel guilty in regard to things you shouldn't feel guilty, and not feel guilty in regard to things you should feel guilty.

43
00:04:03,739 --> 00:04:04,459
[SPEAKER_02]: It's going to come up.

44
00:04:04,806 --> 00:04:07,888
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the kind of the main trait of an untamed mind.

45
00:04:08,989 --> 00:04:14,394
[SPEAKER_02]: And it comes up once you take on the virtue, take on the sense of strength, start dwelling in solitude.

46
00:04:16,515 --> 00:04:17,616
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's unavoidable.

47
00:04:17,656 --> 00:04:23,581
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like, well, this shouldn't be coming, or I could, if I do something right, this won't be coming up.

48
00:04:24,081 --> 00:04:24,261
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

49
00:04:24,281 --> 00:04:29,786
[SPEAKER_02]: That is the pretty much the main characteristic of an untamed mind.

50
00:04:30,086 --> 00:04:32,608
[SPEAKER_02]: It's this irrational placement of emphasis.

51
00:04:33,331 --> 00:04:35,753
[SPEAKER_02]: Wrong emphasis on wrong things.

52
00:04:35,813 --> 00:04:36,994
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it takes time to undo that.

53
00:04:38,095 --> 00:04:39,816
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why it's important how you go about it.

54
00:04:41,657 --> 00:04:42,158
[SPEAKER_02]: In that sense.

55
00:04:42,999 --> 00:04:45,500
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have that, say, irrational guilt.

56
00:04:47,322 --> 00:04:52,886
[SPEAKER_02]: You feel guilty on account of stuff that's, like, not really unwholesome, not even your fault, in that sense.

57
00:04:52,946 --> 00:04:55,448
[SPEAKER_02]: But that doesn't diminish the fact that the mind is resisting it.

58
00:04:55,989 --> 00:04:58,370
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what... that's the guilt that you feel.

59
00:04:58,611 --> 00:05:02,514
[SPEAKER_02]: It's that blind resistance of an untrained mind.

60
00:05:03,793 --> 00:05:07,214
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, what do you do when that happens?

61
00:05:07,574 --> 00:05:09,195
[SPEAKER_02]: What do you do when you're going through those things?

62
00:05:10,155 --> 00:05:10,935
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you double down?

63
00:05:11,375 --> 00:05:12,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you tighten the restraint?

64
00:05:14,076 --> 00:05:14,816
[SPEAKER_02]: Increase more?

65
00:05:14,916 --> 00:05:15,757
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, yeah, but why?

66
00:05:16,477 --> 00:05:17,957
[SPEAKER_02]: I haven't done anything wrong to begin with.

67
00:05:19,818 --> 00:05:21,398
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I am keeping the virtue.

68
00:05:21,919 --> 00:05:22,819
[SPEAKER_02]: I am restrained.

69
00:05:23,599 --> 00:05:26,660
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not giving in to unwholesome behavior, yet the mind still acts like this.

70
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:29,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do I need to do anything out of it?

71
00:05:29,621 --> 00:05:30,781
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do I need to tighten more?

72
00:05:31,401 --> 00:05:32,102
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, why?

73
00:05:32,522 --> 00:05:33,002
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, because

74
00:05:33,757 --> 00:05:34,358
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't want this.

75
00:05:35,639 --> 00:05:36,399
[SPEAKER_00]: I resist this.

76
00:05:37,641 --> 00:05:46,969
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's kind of like, as I said before, you're trying to train a dog that is unruly, and you are angry at that unruliness.

77
00:05:48,831 --> 00:05:49,771
[SPEAKER_02]: You're upset by it.

78
00:05:49,932 --> 00:05:51,133
[SPEAKER_02]: You're frustrated by it.

79
00:05:52,534 --> 00:05:57,418
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, it's like you're not training a dog anymore.

80
00:05:57,438 --> 00:06:00,281
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just trying to get rid of that unruliness.

81
00:06:02,982 --> 00:06:04,403
[SPEAKER_02]: Pretty much by any means necessary.

82
00:06:05,303 --> 00:06:07,604
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that becomes the focus of your bother.

83
00:06:09,545 --> 00:06:11,846
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's like, no, you have to acknowledge that the dog is wild.

84
00:06:12,746 --> 00:06:13,807
[SPEAKER_02]: That the mind is unruly.

85
00:06:15,507 --> 00:06:18,669
[SPEAKER_02]: And it doesn't... Well, exactly, that you're supposed to tame it.

86
00:06:19,749 --> 00:06:26,492
[SPEAKER_02]: Not, like, conquer it in that sense of just force it down until it listens to you, because you won't.

87
00:06:27,452 --> 00:06:31,474
[SPEAKER_02]: And even if you succeed in, like, pinning it down, well,

88
00:06:31,773 --> 00:06:32,914
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't hold it forever.

89
00:06:33,054 --> 00:06:39,719
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you unpin it, it's going to go back to its wild ways, because it still hasn't really... you haven't been communicating with it.

90
00:06:40,580 --> 00:06:46,624
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no... there is no instruction sort of taking place.

91
00:06:46,764 --> 00:06:49,647
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no conveying of the message to that mind.

92
00:06:51,188 --> 00:06:53,409
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, sure, sometimes it's like really unruly.

93
00:06:54,250 --> 00:06:55,431
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to just pin it down.

94
00:06:55,811 --> 00:06:56,412
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, of course.

95
00:06:56,592 --> 00:06:58,914
[SPEAKER_02]: But I think more often than not, that's not the case.

96
00:06:58,994 --> 00:07:00,715
[SPEAKER_02]: It's more like it's unruly.

97
00:07:01,538 --> 00:07:02,259
[SPEAKER_02]: You're frustrated.

98
00:07:02,359 --> 00:07:03,059
[SPEAKER_02]: It's unpleasant.

99
00:07:03,179 --> 00:07:07,702
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't want to be facing that unpleasantness of having to experience the unruly mind.

100
00:07:08,343 --> 00:07:10,324
[SPEAKER_02]: So, let me do this practice.

101
00:07:10,384 --> 00:07:11,625
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me do this reflection.

102
00:07:11,665 --> 00:07:12,926
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me double down this way.

103
00:07:12,946 --> 00:07:19,070
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me... All while overlooking the root intention behind all of that that you want to do.

104
00:07:19,190 --> 00:07:22,732
[SPEAKER_02]: It's, oh, I really resist this unruliness.

105
00:07:23,433 --> 00:07:24,293
[SPEAKER_02]: I really dislike it.

106
00:07:24,414 --> 00:07:25,654
[SPEAKER_02]: I crave for it to go away.

107
00:07:27,195 --> 00:07:29,457
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's already a wrong message you're conveying to the mind.

108
00:07:30,225 --> 00:07:31,927
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why there is no communication yet.

109
00:07:32,427 --> 00:07:33,068
[SPEAKER_02]: Or very little.

110
00:07:35,490 --> 00:07:38,373
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when the mind is unruly, you have to recognize it as an unruly mind.

111
00:07:39,093 --> 00:07:40,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Like how Satipaṭṭhāna described it.

112
00:07:42,196 --> 00:07:42,737
[SPEAKER_02]: So, okay.

113
00:07:42,817 --> 00:07:45,900
[SPEAKER_02]: Today, dog is very susceptible to my instructions.

114
00:07:47,741 --> 00:07:48,342
[SPEAKER_02]: He listens.

115
00:07:49,042 --> 00:07:50,163
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't even have to tell him twice.

116
00:07:50,784 --> 00:07:52,986
[SPEAKER_02]: Some other day, like I'm a stranger.

117
00:07:55,523 --> 00:08:00,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, if I get frustrated or related, I will lose the sight of where the communication takes place.

118
00:08:01,925 --> 00:08:12,730
[SPEAKER_02]: So, on the days when it doesn't listen, I have to recognize, okay, yes, it would have been great if we continued learning at the same pace like we did last week, but it's not up to me to decide that.

119
00:08:13,590 --> 00:08:14,950
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now, you have to adapt.

120
00:08:15,831 --> 00:08:21,393
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to pick the signs and features of the mind, not tailor it how you want it so that you can teach it.

121
00:08:28,346 --> 00:08:29,627
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you recognize it's unruly.

122
00:08:29,787 --> 00:08:37,811
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, which means it's going to have a very limited amount of messages I can convey, if any.

123
00:08:39,592 --> 00:08:42,674
[SPEAKER_02]: But what do you do when there is an unruly dog that you're trying to train?

124
00:08:44,075 --> 00:08:49,438
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you stop wasting your time trying to communicate during that period of time, because it's not listening.

125
00:08:49,518 --> 00:08:51,379
[SPEAKER_02]: But at the same time, you don't let it run wild.

126
00:08:52,139 --> 00:08:57,682
[SPEAKER_02]: You at least contain it and wait for it to calm down without losing sight of it.

127
00:08:59,832 --> 00:09:03,913
[SPEAKER_02]: And when it calms down, then, you convey the message.

128
00:09:08,794 --> 00:09:09,335
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's kind of...

129
00:09:09,835 --> 00:09:14,416
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, you're training the animal by training yourself, really, in regard to it.

130
00:09:16,516 --> 00:09:19,057
[SPEAKER_02]: By recognizing, hold on, but I can't be holding this attitude.

131
00:09:20,878 --> 00:09:23,038
[SPEAKER_02]: If I want to train a dog, I have to abandon this.

132
00:09:23,498 --> 00:09:24,319
[SPEAKER_02]: I have to upgrade this.

133
00:09:24,459 --> 00:09:24,979
[SPEAKER_02]: I have to change.

134
00:09:24,999 --> 00:09:25,379
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't.

135
00:09:25,599 --> 00:09:27,479
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, this is the message it's getting.

136
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:29,660
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter what I say on top of it.

137
00:09:30,388 --> 00:09:34,871
[SPEAKER_02]: If I'm frustrated, if I'm trying to get rid of it, that is the vibe speaking from me.

138
00:09:34,971 --> 00:09:38,974
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if that's not what I really believe I want, that is what I do.

139
00:09:40,616 --> 00:09:41,917
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what the animal's gonna see.

140
00:09:45,339 --> 00:09:59,790
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's why I keep saying to people it's important, because if you assume this external view, that saying no to everything is always better, then you're not gonna see this this whole field of development that's kind of in between.

141
00:10:00,717 --> 00:10:10,760
[SPEAKER_02]: Because every time there is the unruly mind, any time there is a resistance comes up from your end, you just double down then and say no to more things, so that this won't happen.

142
00:10:12,021 --> 00:10:28,026
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's almost like, yeah, it's kind of still carries a certain implications, not necessarily of the full-blown Sīla Bhāgavata Paramāsa, but it has certain, like a same principle of that magical kind of, okay, so if I do better in these other ways, this won't come up.

143
00:10:30,059 --> 00:10:31,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there is a grain of truth in that.

144
00:10:32,741 --> 00:10:38,106
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you could review your behavior and see, is the mind unruly because I've been careless?

145
00:10:40,327 --> 00:10:45,071
[SPEAKER_02]: And if the answer is no, or there's nothing obvious that you know of, then it's probably not.

146
00:10:45,131 --> 00:10:47,513
[SPEAKER_02]: Then it's unruly because it still needs time to learn.

147
00:10:48,814 --> 00:10:55,140
[SPEAKER_02]: But your frustration, your doubling down, you're trying to really speed it up, well, that's not going to help.

148
00:10:55,940 --> 00:10:56,921
[SPEAKER_02]: That's going to slow it down.

149
00:10:59,394 --> 00:11:12,759
[SPEAKER_02]: Same, like, your strings were too loose, you tighten them, you start tightening them, you reach the kind of right tune, and then you think, well, tightening brought the right tune.

150
00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:16,061
[SPEAKER_02]: So, let me, therefore, tighten even more.

151
00:11:17,281 --> 00:11:18,382
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you lose the right tune.

152
00:11:20,042 --> 00:11:21,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Tightening is the thing to do.

153
00:11:21,863 --> 00:11:22,203
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

154
00:11:22,463 --> 00:11:25,865
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if I do tightening... because tightening, factually, brought the right tune.

155
00:11:27,105 --> 00:11:28,486
[SPEAKER_02]: But only because things were too loose.

156
00:11:29,631 --> 00:11:33,254
[SPEAKER_02]: But now the point comes where tightening takes you away from it, you know.

157
00:11:38,219 --> 00:11:54,254
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, for example, you have... you think like, oh, I have a good sex train, or whatever example you want to take, and then you feel... you feel bad, because you had, like, a...

158
00:11:56,148 --> 00:11:58,690
[SPEAKER_02]: Seemingly conceited thought of yourself.

159
00:11:59,570 --> 00:12:01,111
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I have such a good sense of shame.

160
00:12:01,131 --> 00:12:02,913
[SPEAKER_02]: I have a very good samādhi.

161
00:12:04,994 --> 00:12:06,935
[SPEAKER_02]: And then it's like, no, I have no samādhi.

162
00:12:06,955 --> 00:12:08,176
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you start hating yourself.

163
00:12:09,317 --> 00:12:10,558
[SPEAKER_02]: You start self-loathing it.

164
00:12:14,340 --> 00:12:17,062
[SPEAKER_02]: What happens when you're self-loathing?

165
00:12:20,024 --> 00:12:20,905
[SPEAKER_02]: How does that come to be?

166
00:12:25,912 --> 00:12:26,753
[SPEAKER_02]: What is self-loathing?

167
00:12:28,734 --> 00:12:30,356
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you really loathe yourself?

168
00:12:31,437 --> 00:12:31,617
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

169
00:12:33,238 --> 00:12:34,459
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, there is self-loathing.

170
00:12:37,081 --> 00:12:37,662
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how come?

171
00:12:40,664 --> 00:12:41,405
[SPEAKER_02]: Who's loathing who?

172
00:12:41,425 --> 00:12:53,895
[SPEAKER_01]: I was going to say, is it just another form of resisting the mind being untamed,

173
00:12:55,984 --> 00:13:00,426
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you have plenty of resistances, but they don't necessarily mean you're self-loathing.

174
00:13:00,826 --> 00:13:01,747
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not hating yourself.

175
00:13:07,129 --> 00:13:08,530
[SPEAKER_02]: Where does the loathing come from?

176
00:13:33,662 --> 00:13:38,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the same place that the kind of self-congratulating came from.

177
00:13:39,004 --> 00:13:39,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is?

178
00:13:42,306 --> 00:13:42,726
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind.

179
00:13:43,947 --> 00:13:44,347
[SPEAKER_02]: The animal.

180
00:13:44,507 --> 00:13:45,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the animal.

181
00:13:45,327 --> 00:13:46,528
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

182
00:13:46,548 --> 00:13:47,648
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

183
00:13:47,668 --> 00:13:47,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

184
00:13:49,109 --> 00:13:51,510
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the animal hates you.

185
00:13:55,732 --> 00:14:01,955
[SPEAKER_02]: And when it does, you ask yourself, is it because of something I've done?

186
00:14:02,919 --> 00:14:10,884
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, have I really acted in an intentionally unwholesome way, so when the mind turned against me, he has every right to do so.

187
00:14:13,965 --> 00:14:28,453
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, as I said, if you're established in virtue, sense of strength, not even like perfect, perfect, you're gonna have your mind loathing you, without you having done anything wrong.

188
00:14:28,473 --> 00:14:29,334
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's unpleasant.

189
00:14:33,535 --> 00:14:37,258
[SPEAKER_02]: It's unpleasant to have your own mind basically loathing you.

190
00:14:38,438 --> 00:14:44,342
[SPEAKER_02]: And, eh... not seeing that, you're experiencing self-loathing, to put it that way.

191
00:14:44,402 --> 00:14:46,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, it's not you who is loathing yourself.

192
00:14:47,424 --> 00:14:50,206
[SPEAKER_02]: It's you who is taking the mind or the face value of loathing you.

193
00:14:53,869 --> 00:15:01,714
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's... that's that deeply ingrained resistance, that it's like you can know that you haven't done anything wrong,

194
00:15:03,410 --> 00:15:09,735
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind still feels the same towards you, at least for that period of time, and you still feel valid.

195
00:15:10,016 --> 00:15:11,237
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, okay, so I have to change this.

196
00:15:13,719 --> 00:15:18,723
[SPEAKER_02]: You feel valid, validated in those efforts and acceptance of that loathing.

197
00:15:23,507 --> 00:15:26,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you have like a

198
00:15:28,655 --> 00:15:35,078
[SPEAKER_00]: A sense of self-praising or self-congratulation, that's also from the mind.

199
00:15:35,178 --> 00:15:35,998
[SPEAKER_01]: It's also from the mind.

200
00:15:36,058 --> 00:15:37,599
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're jumping on that bandwagon immediately.

201
00:15:37,619 --> 00:15:39,540
[SPEAKER_00]: It does the same thing, for the same reason.

202
00:15:40,240 --> 00:15:40,500
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

203
00:15:42,481 --> 00:15:43,661
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, wow, I'm so amazing.

204
00:15:44,142 --> 00:15:56,447
[SPEAKER_02]: The thought comes up, prompted by the animal, for whatever reason, and it's like, okay, factually, it might be true there and then, because of something I've done, or because I've done nothing.

205
00:15:56,627 --> 00:15:57,187
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind just

206
00:15:58,637 --> 00:16:07,079
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, it's not up to me to take it at face value and run with it, through either blindly accepting it or blindly resisting it.

207
00:16:07,939 --> 00:16:26,422
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the point is, even if you're doing everything right, in terms of the gradual training, there will be some overlap that you will have to grind through, in a sense of overlap, that you're doing everything right, yet the mind is still loading you, for whatever reason, whatever day,

208
00:16:27,312 --> 00:16:28,393
[SPEAKER_02]: For however long.

209
00:16:30,093 --> 00:16:34,956
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's instead of, oh, but let me change my behavior, let me improve it, let me this...

210
00:16:35,656 --> 00:16:45,101
[SPEAKER_02]: If you already know that it's nothing... it's not because of anything obvious that you've done, then it's like, no, it's just the mind needs to get the message that I'm not following along with this anymore.

211
00:16:46,022 --> 00:16:48,403
[SPEAKER_02]: Without trying to kill it, beat it, strangle it.

212
00:16:48,703 --> 00:16:51,564
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, obviously, that's giving it too much of a message.

213
00:16:51,584 --> 00:16:56,647
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, yeah, the dog might just hate you on some days.

214
00:16:58,793 --> 00:17:04,935
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's like, oh, I haven't done anything, even accidentally, let alone intentionally.

215
00:17:05,015 --> 00:17:10,716
[SPEAKER_02]: So, okay, you just have to live through that and calm down the animal and then continue.

216
00:17:12,717 --> 00:17:19,458
[SPEAKER_02]: But, obviously, people not seeing the signs of their own mind, not seeing their own mind, whatever happens in their mind, it's theirs.

217
00:17:19,638 --> 00:17:20,518
[SPEAKER_02]: It's they're doing it.

218
00:17:20,759 --> 00:17:23,799
[SPEAKER_02]: Although, see, nobody wants to love themselves.

219
00:17:23,919 --> 00:17:25,540
[SPEAKER_02]: Nobody can love themselves in that sense.

220
00:17:25,600 --> 00:17:26,340
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, they are.

221
00:17:27,195 --> 00:17:30,337
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the mind is, and they're fully identified with it, because they don't see anything in between.

222
00:17:32,278 --> 00:17:37,261
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, whichever kind of whimsical direction that animal takes...

223
00:17:37,281 --> 00:17:37,681
[SPEAKER_00]: It's yours.

224
00:17:37,761 --> 00:17:38,822
[SPEAKER_02]: It's you doing it.

225
00:17:39,442 --> 00:17:40,102
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't even know...

226
00:17:40,243 --> 00:17:41,123
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't want it yet.

227
00:17:41,283 --> 00:17:42,524
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm doing it and I can't stop it.

228
00:17:49,688 --> 00:17:51,449
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why... pretty much why...

229
00:17:52,770 --> 00:18:20,733
[SPEAKER_02]: Meditation is what it is today, contemporarily speaking, because, you know, as I said, there is there is no development whatsoever to begin with, and then all people are left with is just, okay, I can't cope with this anymore, so let me just find ways of dumbing it all down, completely, sort of, having half asleep, stop thinking, stop everything, just to experience some form of artificial peace, where I become unaware of the animal that hates me.

230
00:18:21,705 --> 00:18:22,865
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, I'm identified with.

231
00:18:26,866 --> 00:18:33,728
[SPEAKER_02]: Or you could reason and go through the motions of psychologizations and just repeated steps.

232
00:18:34,788 --> 00:18:35,368
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

233
00:18:35,628 --> 00:18:36,288
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

234
00:18:36,948 --> 00:18:37,508
[SPEAKER_02]: Why it is?

235
00:18:37,568 --> 00:18:38,248
[SPEAKER_02]: Why it isn't?

236
00:18:38,308 --> 00:18:39,189
[SPEAKER_02]: Why it shouldn't be?

237
00:18:39,229 --> 00:18:39,749
[SPEAKER_02]: Why at all?

238
00:18:40,769 --> 00:18:42,529
[SPEAKER_02]: All rooted in, yeah, but see, hold on.

239
00:18:43,570 --> 00:18:46,450
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do I need to do anything when I can't do this animal being an idiot?

240
00:18:49,551 --> 00:18:50,231
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like I don't.

241
00:18:50,662 --> 00:18:53,423
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I have to draw a line for myself to stop kind of giving in to that.

242
00:18:53,483 --> 00:18:54,743
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, yeah, let me do this.

243
00:18:57,144 --> 00:18:59,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, if it loads you, fine.

244
00:19:00,945 --> 00:19:03,806
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not fine if it loads you because you did something stupid.

245
00:19:05,046 --> 00:19:06,466
[SPEAKER_02]: But then you'll know that very clearly.

246
00:19:08,347 --> 00:19:13,508
[SPEAKER_01]: That's, again, that sense of shame will be very, very obvious if it was broken.

247
00:19:15,469 --> 00:19:19,450
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, if you take the mind at face value, it will present you with completely

248
00:19:20,545 --> 00:19:24,207
[SPEAKER_02]: Faultless, blameless scenarios, and blame you for it.

249
00:19:24,888 --> 00:19:25,748
[SPEAKER_02]: And you'll feel the guilt.

250
00:19:26,769 --> 00:19:27,490
[SPEAKER_02]: Irrational guilt.

251
00:19:27,730 --> 00:19:28,230
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll feel it.

252
00:19:29,811 --> 00:19:36,095
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you think, okay, so every... this... every guilt must be dealt with, you're falling for its trap.

253
00:19:41,959 --> 00:19:49,424
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the more familiar you are with the mind, doing that... Yeah.

254
00:19:50,348 --> 00:19:50,808
[SPEAKER_02]: The better.

255
00:19:51,149 --> 00:19:58,034
[SPEAKER_02]: But the familiarity of the mind doing that is not on the level of theory and reasoning.

256
00:19:58,374 --> 00:20:04,578
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, you need to learn about it, but really familiarity is through not acting out of, no matter what the mind pressures you with.

257
00:20:05,079 --> 00:20:13,765
[SPEAKER_02]: You're becoming more and more accustomed to, more and more familiar, more and more, like, wise, in that sense.

258
00:20:13,785 --> 00:20:16,807
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the suttas, I think it's the Agama, they said,

259
00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:20,242
[SPEAKER_02]: Wisdom is the absence of dispassion, basically.

260
00:20:21,022 --> 00:20:21,763
[SPEAKER_00]: The absence of passion.

261
00:20:22,363 --> 00:20:23,784
[SPEAKER_02]: No, sorry, the absence of passion, yeah.

262
00:20:24,765 --> 00:20:27,747
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the disappearance of passion equals wisdom.

263
00:20:28,047 --> 00:20:33,071
[SPEAKER_02]: Not having it all fit in your little mind, like, you know, all the theories and stuff.

264
00:20:33,091 --> 00:20:34,192
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all nice and connected.

265
00:20:35,052 --> 00:20:36,173
[SPEAKER_02]: It matches every sutta.

266
00:20:36,193 --> 00:20:36,914
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, no, no.

267
00:20:37,694 --> 00:20:41,757
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize you could be telling yourself all the right answers and they will not bring dispassion.

268
00:20:42,578 --> 00:20:45,620
[SPEAKER_02]: If, however, you don't bother with any answering, for whatever reason,

269
00:20:46,276 --> 00:20:48,578
[SPEAKER_02]: But, you do not give in to an unruly animal.

270
00:20:49,018 --> 00:20:54,483
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter what it pressures you, doesn't matter how personal it tries to get, you are developing this passion.

271
00:20:54,523 --> 00:20:58,386
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's the essence of this passion, is not acting out of the passion in mind.

272
00:20:59,186 --> 00:21:00,527
[SPEAKER_00]: And developing wisdom at the same time.

273
00:21:00,767 --> 00:21:02,048
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's what wisdom is, yeah.

274
00:21:05,651 --> 00:21:07,293
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, by all means, become familiar with it.

275
00:21:08,794 --> 00:21:15,099
[SPEAKER_02]: Through not acting out and through recognizing the peripheral mental states, the moods,

276
00:21:16,493 --> 00:21:21,097
[SPEAKER_02]: That animal is in the pressure that it exerts on you, sometimes subtle, sometimes coarse.

277
00:21:22,038 --> 00:21:32,767
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way you see it, it's like, okay, losing sight of this, in other words, like forgetting about it, is how I make the mistake of sort of going along with it.

278
00:21:33,728 --> 00:21:37,791
[SPEAKER_02]: But if I don't lose a sight of it, whatever it's revolving around, it's still not a problem.

279
00:21:38,071 --> 00:21:42,015
[SPEAKER_02]: It still hasn't taken the center stage, as we spoke before.

280
00:21:46,429 --> 00:22:07,314
[SPEAKER_00]: It reminds me of that taking that movement of the mind for granted as yours, your point of view, because that has enormous consequences over...

281
00:22:07,594 --> 00:22:10,875
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that's basically a saṃsara, really, but like...

282
00:22:13,206 --> 00:22:40,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Like an example, I was thinking about recently, it reminded me, like, a long time ago, somebody I was speaking to with, this was in the place where I was before, we were having a conversation and she said, you know, like, I think I really started thinking about what it would be like to experience non-self,

283
00:22:42,190 --> 00:22:43,792
[SPEAKER_00]: And to experience complete dispassion.

284
00:22:44,292 --> 00:22:46,914
[SPEAKER_00]: And I realized I'm terrified of it.

285
00:22:47,135 --> 00:22:47,775
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want it.

286
00:22:48,176 --> 00:22:49,537
[SPEAKER_02]: She got the right idea.

287
00:22:49,897 --> 00:22:50,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

288
00:22:50,177 --> 00:22:50,958
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was the point.

289
00:22:50,998 --> 00:22:51,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And she felt fear.

290
00:22:52,299 --> 00:22:52,560
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

291
00:22:52,640 --> 00:22:55,763
[SPEAKER_00]: But then she said, I don't want it.

292
00:22:56,263 --> 00:22:56,924
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what to do.

293
00:22:57,164 --> 00:23:01,908
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I just... And then she said, and she literally, like, blocked.

294
00:23:02,268 --> 00:23:02,869
[SPEAKER_00]: That was okay.

295
00:23:02,889 --> 00:23:06,953
[SPEAKER_00]: So I have to try and make myself want it in order to then practice for it.

296
00:23:08,488 --> 00:23:20,278
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was thinking, like, I remember thinking at the time, that's very interesting, because I had recognized, I had loads of times, like, thinking about it and feeling fear, like, I'm terrified of becoming an arahant.

297
00:23:21,138 --> 00:23:21,659
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to.

298
00:23:22,279 --> 00:23:29,925
[SPEAKER_00]: But then I would think, well, why should I take that as the measure, as the reference point for what is good?

299
00:23:30,586 --> 00:23:34,990
[SPEAKER_00]: When does what I want, when has what I want, doing what I want to do, brought

300
00:23:36,247 --> 00:23:38,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Happiness and lasting peace.

301
00:23:38,289 --> 00:23:39,871
[SPEAKER_00]: It hasn't.

302
00:23:39,911 --> 00:23:42,694
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you end up realizing, well, actually, this is the only way.

303
00:23:42,714 --> 00:23:47,239
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to do it, in spite of, at the same time, recognizing, oh, I don't want to.

304
00:23:47,939 --> 00:23:49,061
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind doesn't want to.

305
00:23:49,341 --> 00:23:49,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

306
00:23:51,323 --> 00:23:51,583
[SPEAKER_00]: But...

307
00:23:54,787 --> 00:24:01,412
[SPEAKER_00]: And even, like, just basic sense of shame can kind of help with that, in a way.

308
00:24:02,152 --> 00:24:05,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, just that would kind of put a block.

309
00:24:06,095 --> 00:24:11,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I think about, you know, I don't want to, so maybe I'll just, like, abandon it.

310
00:24:11,939 --> 00:24:13,280
[SPEAKER_00]: And you feel a sense of shame.

311
00:24:13,360 --> 00:24:16,763
[SPEAKER_02]: No, like... No, that's not a right thing to do.

312
00:24:16,783 --> 00:24:18,064
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not a right thing to do.

313
00:24:18,144 --> 00:24:19,285
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't want to quit.

314
00:24:19,305 --> 00:24:20,025
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't.

315
00:24:20,165 --> 00:24:21,626
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't let myself just quit.

316
00:24:21,646 --> 00:24:22,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know...

317
00:24:24,230 --> 00:24:28,631
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, other people, people in Buddha's time, all these people, you see, they were struggling and so on.

318
00:24:28,671 --> 00:24:30,852
[SPEAKER_00]: If they could do it, I must be able to do too.

319
00:24:30,892 --> 00:24:41,174
[SPEAKER_02]: There's just this basic... That's the difference between sort of doing it already for the more transparent, authentic reasons, as opposed to doing it just because you were inspired on a kind of wrong idea.

320
00:24:41,254 --> 00:24:41,414
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

321
00:24:42,334 --> 00:24:44,835
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I think I was saying before, like, even when I was...

322
00:24:45,735 --> 00:24:46,395
[SPEAKER_02]: So I was...

323
00:24:46,415 --> 00:24:52,717
[SPEAKER_02]: I wanted to become a monk and I was already prepared, writing to monasteries and stuff, and then I had that interview for the UK.

324
00:24:53,441 --> 00:24:55,662
[SPEAKER_02]: Visa, pretty much the only place they replied to me.

325
00:24:56,903 --> 00:24:57,603
[SPEAKER_02]: And they denied me.

326
00:24:58,583 --> 00:24:58,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

327
00:24:58,924 --> 00:25:15,111
[SPEAKER_02]: I went there, and I went through it, and he said, sorry, like, you know, if you were already a monk or something, I could let you in, because that's the category, but a non-member of religious order, to go in to become a member of religious order, I can't give you the member of religious order visa, because it's simply not true.

328
00:25:15,812 --> 00:25:16,732
[SPEAKER_02]: And I remember I was relieved.

329
00:25:17,372 --> 00:25:18,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

330
00:25:18,293 --> 00:25:21,995
[SPEAKER_02]: I was going home, feeling like, oh, wait, it's off me now, because I genuinely tried.

331
00:25:23,414 --> 00:25:33,222
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I remember I was scared after that thought, because I thought, oh, so then, like, shit, if I'm relieved, means I have to, like, fight now twice as hard to not slide back.

332
00:25:34,003 --> 00:25:36,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Because before, at least, I had something that was sort of heading towards.

333
00:25:38,166 --> 00:25:43,831
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, like, three or four days, and he called me, said, come on, I found the category I can slot you in.

334
00:25:44,562 --> 00:25:47,283
[SPEAKER_02]: And then it was like back on, that, okay, we're doing this.

335
00:25:47,824 --> 00:25:51,505
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, if I were to just let my mind decide, I would not have done it.

336
00:25:51,565 --> 00:25:52,566
[SPEAKER_02]: I had no reason to do it.

337
00:25:53,126 --> 00:25:56,468
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, there was nothing that I was running away from or bothering, quite the opposite.

338
00:25:57,028 --> 00:26:00,570
[SPEAKER_02]: But deep down, you realize, yeah, but how long will these things last?

339
00:26:01,350 --> 00:26:05,712
[SPEAKER_02]: How long will... before it changes the way I don't want it to change?

340
00:26:05,752 --> 00:26:08,154
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's like, it's like an obvious truth that's right there.

341
00:26:08,194 --> 00:26:10,575
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, oh, yeah, nothing else is really worth

342
00:26:12,906 --> 00:26:23,773
[SPEAKER_02]: But if, say, if your practice is just rooted in that inspiration, which it might be fine in the beginning, like that's the only way you know, but you will come to the point where exactly you realize the mind doesn't want it.

343
00:26:25,094 --> 00:26:32,358
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you still use your inspiration as a criteria for making a judgment of right and wrong, you're done.

344
00:26:33,619 --> 00:26:40,103
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you'll be hopping from one practice to another, one place to another, this, that, and then whatever takes your fancy.

345
00:26:40,935 --> 00:26:46,037
[SPEAKER_02]: Which usually is going to be non-practice, non-virtue, and sensuality, and so on.

346
00:26:47,438 --> 00:26:58,382
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's like... That's why, like, you really, like, through correct recollection, reflection, and drilling in this context, you are clarifying stuff.

347
00:26:58,722 --> 00:27:05,625
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even when the mind doesn't want to do it, the broader picture still, kind of, does not, sort of, sway because of it.

348
00:27:05,685 --> 00:27:09,607
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, yeah, it doesn't want to do it, but what else is there to do?

349
00:27:10,982 --> 00:27:12,443
[SPEAKER_02]: Other than tame your mind rightly.

350
00:27:13,084 --> 00:27:17,848
[SPEAKER_02]: Because whatever I would let my mind decide to do, it's because it wants the peace.

351
00:27:18,509 --> 00:27:19,390
[SPEAKER_02]: It wants the happiness.

352
00:27:20,050 --> 00:27:24,474
[SPEAKER_02]: It just obviously doesn't know how to get it in that wise way.

353
00:27:24,734 --> 00:27:26,115
[SPEAKER_02]: It would go ignorantly about it.

354
00:27:26,216 --> 00:27:33,382
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if I allow it to go in ignorant ways about those, seeking those things, like, I'm the one who's gonna suffer, and the mind will suffer.

355
00:27:34,943 --> 00:27:37,566
[SPEAKER_02]: So, but that perspective, like, yes, it makes sense.

356
00:27:38,752 --> 00:27:46,193
[SPEAKER_02]: You can read it, you can hear it, you can understand it, you can even believe in it, but you need to be, you know, maintaining that to really sink in.

357
00:27:46,414 --> 00:27:52,215
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, when the mind does become unruly, becomes like this, becomes like that, this practice has weight.

358
00:27:53,135 --> 00:27:54,995
[SPEAKER_02]: The perspective, sorry, has weight, has value.

359
00:27:57,696 --> 00:28:01,817
[SPEAKER_02]: Because otherwise, yeah, it's just hollow words, really.

360
00:28:03,137 --> 00:28:03,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Empty ideas.

361
00:28:05,026 --> 00:28:12,990
[SPEAKER_01]: I've thought of the virtue of the right kind of stubbornness, almost.

362
00:28:13,551 --> 00:28:13,831
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

363
00:28:14,271 --> 00:28:15,452
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's applied rightly, yes.

364
00:28:15,552 --> 00:28:15,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

365
00:28:16,232 --> 00:28:22,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Just like stubbornness against your own mind's tides.

366
00:28:29,659 --> 00:28:34,702
[SPEAKER_02]: There is that movement of the mind, movement of the animal, when it sort of twitches,

367
00:28:36,160 --> 00:28:36,780
[SPEAKER_02]: calls away.

368
00:28:39,142 --> 00:28:42,184
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, you can't help but overlook it.

369
00:28:44,405 --> 00:28:46,626
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you see it, you can't help but overlook it.

370
00:28:48,808 --> 00:28:54,311
[SPEAKER_02]: And by that I mean, you can't help but be identified with it first, take it for granted.

371
00:28:54,451 --> 00:28:55,872
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, okay, this is the animal.

372
00:28:56,713 --> 00:28:58,754
[SPEAKER_02]: Once you're ready, it's like, oh, hold on.

373
00:29:00,055 --> 00:29:01,316
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter the movement.

374
00:29:02,476 --> 00:29:03,437
[SPEAKER_02]: It belongs to the animal.

375
00:29:13,313 --> 00:29:20,716
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's maintaining that perspective, not acting out, and becoming more and more familiar with these movements of the animal.

376
00:29:21,517 --> 00:29:25,899
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, one day, you just realize, oh, it moved, and I haven't identified myself with it.

377
00:29:28,580 --> 00:29:32,942
[SPEAKER_02]: It sounds like something you can just pre-decide when it's gonna... when you're not gonna be identified.

378
00:29:33,442 --> 00:29:41,046
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you keep recognizing that, yes, the re... the sole reason I am feeling the pressure of the animal,

379
00:29:43,345 --> 00:30:00,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Although I'm not making it worse or aggravating it through acting out, the fact that I am being pressured to act out means that somewhere underneath that, the Buddha way, I have taken it up.

380
00:30:00,971 --> 00:30:02,072
[SPEAKER_02]: I am identified with it.

381
00:30:05,193 --> 00:30:08,854
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not something you can just travel back, undo that part, and then everything else falls in place.

382
00:30:09,194 --> 00:30:11,415
[SPEAKER_02]: You can only do it in reverse, so to speak.

383
00:30:12,142 --> 00:30:18,287
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're doing it more accurately, not by trying not to take up the movement through reasoning.

384
00:30:18,307 --> 00:30:23,731
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you're doing it more accurately through not giving in to the pressure and conveying that message to the mind.

385
00:30:25,293 --> 00:30:32,779
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, the more it moves in the face of that clear message, the clearer the movement becomes, as if something that it's not you're doing.

386
00:30:33,119 --> 00:30:34,380
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter which way it goes.

387
00:30:36,722 --> 00:30:37,723
[SPEAKER_02]: And that it cannot touch you.

388
00:30:38,223 --> 00:30:38,824
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the point.

389
00:30:39,244 --> 00:30:40,125
[SPEAKER_02]: Basically, you flinch.

390
00:30:41,193 --> 00:30:43,374
[SPEAKER_02]: The animal does that to your face and you flinch.

391
00:30:43,994 --> 00:30:45,415
[SPEAKER_02]: And you just can't... it's like a reflex.

392
00:30:46,835 --> 00:30:56,379
[SPEAKER_02]: But you realize the only... the reason you still have that reflex is because you still are not 100% clear that it cannot touch you, doesn't matter how forcefully it comes at you.

393
00:30:58,860 --> 00:31:01,782
[SPEAKER_02]: But the clearer that becomes, the less of a flinch you're going to have.

394
00:31:02,982 --> 00:31:04,123
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the mind moves quickly.

395
00:31:05,203 --> 00:31:07,704
[SPEAKER_02]: It makes no difference to you, because it moves elsewhere.

396
00:31:07,924 --> 00:31:09,525
[SPEAKER_02]: It is elsewhere before it moves,

397
00:31:10,114 --> 00:31:14,816
[SPEAKER_02]: And its movement remains elsewhere, not where it can harm you.

398
00:31:15,696 --> 00:31:16,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

399
00:31:16,136 --> 00:31:34,902
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, by not flinching, you have your eyes wide open, you can see the whole thing clearly, and you recognize... Actually, you cannot touch, but it's like... Well, the recognition of you cannot be touched starts way before you are able to not flinch.

400
00:31:35,022 --> 00:31:38,443
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll be flinching, but through not acting out,

401
00:31:39,518 --> 00:31:47,041
[SPEAKER_02]: Through recognizing that pressure, although you flinched, you recognize, but hold on, it still cannot do anything unless I act out of it.

402
00:31:47,682 --> 00:31:55,265
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how it starts to sink in and comes to the point of where even the split-second animal twitch does not make you flinch.

403
00:31:57,366 --> 00:31:58,346
[SPEAKER_02]: Cannot make you flinch.

404
00:31:58,927 --> 00:32:03,088
[SPEAKER_02]: Not because you're so focused on it to not flinch and prop your eyes open or something.

405
00:32:04,089 --> 00:32:07,250
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not a... it's not a... it's not rooted in, like, determination.

406
00:32:07,931 --> 00:32:08,731
[SPEAKER_02]: It's rooted in just

407
00:32:10,290 --> 00:32:26,733
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding sinking deep enough, whereby it doesn't matter how quick, how sudden, how spontaneous, how unexpected the movement of the animal is, there is absolutely no wavering in the understanding that you're untouchable by that movement.

408
00:32:27,894 --> 00:32:29,114
[SPEAKER_02]: And everything else is second to it.

409
00:32:33,875 --> 00:32:35,675
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's the sole reason you wouldn't be flinching.

410
00:32:37,476 --> 00:32:39,496
[SPEAKER_02]: The understanding that it cannot touch you,

411
00:32:40,415 --> 00:32:41,096
[SPEAKER_02]: It's deep enough.

412
00:32:41,656 --> 00:32:43,458
[SPEAKER_02]: It has settled in far enough.

413
00:32:44,338 --> 00:32:45,920
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the sole reason you would be flinching.

414
00:32:47,641 --> 00:32:56,649
[SPEAKER_02]: Is if you still, at some point, at some corner of your mind, are unsure whether it's gonna touch you or not, whether it's gonna hurt you or not, and then you flinch.

415
00:33:01,333 --> 00:33:03,295
[SPEAKER_01]: And getting to that point is just...

416
00:33:03,979 --> 00:33:05,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you said, you can't start down the road.

417
00:33:06,001 --> 00:33:10,184
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, and I'm saying, like, what you should be aiming for, but, yeah, don't fall into view now.

418
00:33:10,204 --> 00:33:15,868
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so let me learn how not to flinch, while still acting, given to the pressure of... that's already the result of the flinch.

419
00:33:16,028 --> 00:33:20,911
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's like, you'll come to that point if you would have been taming the mind rightly.

420
00:33:21,912 --> 00:33:27,216
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just, as I said, you're training yourself as well in recognizing how far the animal can go.

421
00:33:30,158 --> 00:33:32,440
[SPEAKER_02]: And goes the same way when the mind twitches and

422
00:33:33,228 --> 00:33:36,931
[SPEAKER_02]: Jumps and moves towards pleasure.

423
00:33:37,872 --> 00:33:38,292
[SPEAKER_02]: You flinch.

424
00:33:42,435 --> 00:33:43,936
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not only when it turns against you.

425
00:33:44,396 --> 00:33:45,797
[SPEAKER_02]: Sensuality is a flinch already.

426
00:33:46,118 --> 00:33:47,619
[SPEAKER_02]: You're affected by it because you flinched.

427
00:33:48,499 --> 00:33:50,981
[SPEAKER_02]: You were like, oh, it moved.

428
00:33:51,382 --> 00:33:54,524
[SPEAKER_02]: The animal kicked and I flinched and now I'm running with it.

429
00:33:56,465 --> 00:33:57,486
[SPEAKER_02]: So I gotta stop running.

430
00:33:58,847 --> 00:33:59,788
[SPEAKER_02]: Now I have to endure the

431
00:34:02,584 --> 00:34:03,384
[SPEAKER_02]: Kicking in my face.

432
00:34:04,685 --> 00:34:09,787
[SPEAKER_02]: Put up with flinching until I really understand that it cannot harm me.

433
00:34:10,367 --> 00:34:13,248
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, the animal will start to understand and give up trying to kick me.

434
00:34:13,668 --> 00:34:17,129
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it works.

435
00:34:17,189 --> 00:34:19,330
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't need to hit you.

436
00:34:19,350 --> 00:34:20,910
[SPEAKER_02]: It just needs you to move.

437
00:34:21,411 --> 00:34:26,592
[SPEAKER_02]: So, by kind of twitching towards you, you flinched and you moved.

438
00:34:27,273 --> 00:34:30,594
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't... it doesn't need to make a contact.

439
00:34:32,419 --> 00:34:48,342
[SPEAKER_02]: But once you stop moving, because you fully understood that the mind cannot touch you, well, then the mind will eventually give up trying to touch you, because it also understood that the threat of touching you doesn't work anymore, because you are not flinching.

440
00:34:51,523 --> 00:34:59,605
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it needs to be done in reverse, because there's a lot riding on top of that flinch.

441
00:35:00,565 --> 00:35:11,753
[SPEAKER_02]: Layers upon layers upon layers of you just proliferating and building up perceptions to the point of, say, mind gets gets a sudden desire.

442
00:35:12,813 --> 00:35:16,656
[SPEAKER_02]: You flinch, like momentarily you blink, let's say that that way.

443
00:35:17,416 --> 00:35:22,980
[SPEAKER_02]: You open your eyes next second and the next thing you see it's you're full-on riding towards it.

444
00:35:24,521 --> 00:35:26,062
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing else in between, basically.

445
00:35:28,123 --> 00:35:30,344
[SPEAKER_02]: That's your general untrained mind.

446
00:35:30,464 --> 00:35:33,365
[SPEAKER_02]: Sensuality comes to your face.

447
00:35:34,506 --> 00:35:35,026
[SPEAKER_02]: You flinch.

448
00:35:35,806 --> 00:35:37,707
[SPEAKER_02]: As I said, eyes are closed for a millisecond.

449
00:35:37,747 --> 00:35:42,789
[SPEAKER_02]: When you open them, you are already 80% down the rabbit hole of sensuality.

450
00:35:45,290 --> 00:35:48,731
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, yeah, there's a lot packed in that split second.

451
00:35:49,151 --> 00:35:55,654
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the whole undoing on the level of the middle way, the gradual training.

452
00:35:56,851 --> 00:36:00,012
[SPEAKER_02]: So, first and foremost, you simply stop acting out.

453
00:36:00,472 --> 00:36:07,475
[SPEAKER_02]: That still, now you have to bear the whole weight of the threatening animal trying to move you, desire, hindrances.

454
00:36:08,355 --> 00:36:12,457
[SPEAKER_02]: You flinching, are you being affected by it, but not making it worse.

455
00:36:14,197 --> 00:36:17,898
[SPEAKER_02]: Through not making it worse, your perspective broadens.

456
00:36:18,059 --> 00:36:18,819
[SPEAKER_02]: You do it okay.

457
00:36:19,119 --> 00:36:20,239
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, it still touches me.

458
00:36:20,920 --> 00:36:25,021
[SPEAKER_02]: I would really like to not be even touched, because that would be even more peaceful.

459
00:36:25,653 --> 00:36:37,260
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's nowhere near me, just running down in full-on panic mode, or sensual mode, or anger mode, and acting out like a maniac, which then makes the animal even more powerful.

460
00:36:38,161 --> 00:36:55,152
[SPEAKER_02]: And as you keep, as I said, progressing in reverse of the direction of your proliferation, the point comes when, even if the animal flinches, because it's kind of in face of death, and it's last breath, they're being killed,

461
00:36:55,778 --> 00:36:56,659
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't be moved by it.

462
00:36:56,679 --> 00:36:57,839
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't flinch on account of that.

463
00:36:57,939 --> 00:37:02,022
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if the animal twitches and fights for its life, you won't be moved by that.

464
00:37:02,662 --> 00:37:05,104
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why arahant cannot be touched by death either.

465
00:37:06,325 --> 00:37:07,105
[SPEAKER_02]: He's free from it.

466
00:37:09,527 --> 00:37:16,911
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter what animal does, how close it feels, you know it's always gonna stop in front of your feet.

467
00:37:17,011 --> 00:37:18,412
[SPEAKER_02]: Cannot possibly come closer.

468
00:37:18,912 --> 00:37:22,755
[SPEAKER_02]: Impossible because, again, you haven't reasoned it or just believe in it.

469
00:37:23,457 --> 00:37:27,819
[SPEAKER_02]: You've been doing it, and it's been confirmed over and over and over and over and over again.

470
00:37:28,319 --> 00:37:30,960
[SPEAKER_02]: Even the dumbest of the mind will understand it.

471
00:37:31,261 --> 00:37:32,361
[SPEAKER_02]: It will have to sink in.

472
00:37:34,782 --> 00:37:36,303
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the differences in the suttas.

473
00:37:36,323 --> 00:37:40,144
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, somebody needs to do this for a fortnight, and he's an arahant, so he needs to do it for seven years.

474
00:37:40,905 --> 00:37:47,848
[SPEAKER_02]: But either way, even the dumbest of the minds will have to get the message, if you stick to that right level.

